WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.317 --> 00:00:11.553
There was no possibility of taking a walk that day. The cold November wind had brought so much rain that outdoor exercise was out of the question.

2
00:00:11.554 --> 00:00:23.629
My cousins, Eliza, John, and Georgiana Reed, were clustered around their mama in the sitting room. For the moment they were neither quarreling nor crying.

3
00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:34.871
Mrs. Reed looked perfectly happy as she reclined on a sofa by the fireside with her darlings around her. She had excluded me from the group.

4
00:00:34.872 --> 00:00:53.596
"I hate to keep you at a distance, Jane," she said. "But until I hear from the nurse or learn from my own observations that you are honestly trying to acquire a better disposition, I must exclude you from privileges intended only for happy children."

5
00:00:53.597 --> 00:00:57.521
"What does the nurse say I've done?" I asked.

6
00:00:57.522 --> 00:01:08.168
"Jane, I don't like children who ask questions. Be seated somewhere and remain silent until you can speak pleasantly."

7
00:01:08.169 --> 00:01:16.080
A small room adjoined the sitting room. I slipped in there and soon picked out a book from the bookcase.

8
00:01:16.081 --> 00:01:21.238
Climbing onto the window seat, I drew the curtain closed around me.

9
00:01:21.239 --> 00:01:27.991
I feared nothing except interruption, and that came too soon when the door opened.

10
00:01:27.992 --> 00:01:34.498
"Boo!" cried John. Then he paused because the room seemed empty.

11
00:01:34.499 --> 00:01:45.238
"Eliza! Georgiana!" he called to his sisters. "Jane isn't here. Tell Mama she has run out into the rain."

12
00:01:45.239 --> 00:01:54.566
John never would have found me, but Eliza looked into the room and said at once, "She's in the window seat, John."

13
00:01:54.567 --> 00:02:00.628
I came out immediately because I trembled at the idea of John dragging me out.

14
00:02:00.629 --> 00:02:03.628
"What do you want?" I asked.

15
00:02:03.629 --> 00:02:12.123
"Say, 'What do you want, Master Reed?'" he replied. "I want you to come here."

16
00:02:12.124 --> 00:02:22.930
Seating himself in an armchair, he motioned for me to stand before him. John was a 14-year-old schoolboy while I was only 10.

17
00:02:22.931 --> 00:02:31.931
He bullied me constantly, but his mother never noticed, and the servants didn't like to offend him by taking my side.

18
00:02:31.932 --> 00:02:41.917
I approached John, and he spent three minutes sticking out his tongue at me. All at once he hit me so hard that I staggered back.

19
00:02:41.918 --> 00:02:49.585
"That's for your impudence in answering Mama a while ago," he said. "And for sneaking behind the curtain."

20
00:02:49.586 --> 00:02:59.253
Accustomed to his abuse, I had no intention of replying. My main concern was how to endure the blow that would surely follow.

21
00:02:59.254 --> 00:03:03.227
"What were you doing behind the curtain?" John asked.

22
00:03:03.228 --> 00:03:05.010
"Reading."

23
00:03:05.011 --> 00:03:07.430
"Show me the book."

24
00:03:07.431 --> 00:03:11.162
I returned to the window seat and fetched it for him.

25
00:03:11.163 --> 00:03:18.806
"You have no business taking our books. Mama says your father left you no money," said John.

26
00:03:18.807 --> 00:03:27.553
"You ought to be out begging, not living here with gentlemen's children like us, eating our food and wearing clothes at our mother's expense.

27
00:03:27.554 --> 00:03:35.299
I'll teach you not to rummage through my bookshelves. Go stand by the door, away from the mirror and windows."

28
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:41.799
I puzzled over those last directions until I saw him ready to hurl the book at me.

29
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:50.547
I darted aside, but it was too late. The book hit me, and I fell against the door, cutting my head.

30
00:03:50.548 --> 00:03:57.949
"Wicked and cruel boy!" I shouted. "You are like a murderer, a slave driver—"

31
00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:04.100
"What did you say?" he cried. "I'll tell Mama, but first—"

32
00:04:04.101 --> 00:04:09.780
John ran headlong at me, grasping my hair and shoulder.

33
00:04:09.781 --> 00:04:17.933
I don't remember what I did to him, but he bellowed, and Mrs. Reed came running, followed by two servants.

34
00:04:17.934 --> 00:04:23.263
"How could you fly at Master John like that?" said Bessie, the nurse.

35
00:04:23.264 --> 00:04:31.343
"Did anybody ever see such a picture of passion?" said Miss Abbot, who was Mrs. Reed's personal maid.

36
00:04:31.344 --> 00:04:36.594
"Take her away to the red room and lock her in!" ordered Mrs. Reed.

37
00:04:36.595 --> 00:04:41.656
I resisted all the way upstairs, something new for me.

38
00:04:41.657 --> 00:04:51.418
"For shame!" cried Miss Abbot. "What shocking conduct, Miss Jane, to strike a young gentleman, your young master."

39
00:04:51.419 --> 00:05:00.404
"How is he my master?" I asked, still struggling against them as we reached the red room. "Am I a servant?"

40
00:05:00.405 --> 00:05:07.731
"No, you are less than a servant because you do nothing for your keep," said Miss Abbot.

41
00:05:07.732 --> 00:05:11.148
"Sit down and think over your wickedness."

42
00:05:11.149 --> 00:05:17.552
"You ought to be aware, miss, that you are under obligation to Mrs. Reed," said Bessie.

43
00:05:17.553 --> 00:05:23.533
"If she were to turn you out from Gateshead Hall, you'd have to go to the poorhouse."

44
00:05:23.534 --> 00:05:25.961
I had nothing to say.

45
00:05:25.962 --> 00:05:34.449
"If you don't repent," said Miss Abbot, "something bad might be permitted to come down the chimney and fetch you away."

46
00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:39.374
Then they went out, shutting the door and locking it behind them.

47
00:05:39.375 --> 00:05:45.763
The red room was a spare bedroom, very seldom used despite its large size.

48
00:05:45.764 --> 00:05:53.604
Mr. Reed had died in this room nine years ago, and since then few people had crossed its threshold.

49
00:05:53.605 --> 00:06:04.680
I sat on a low ottoman near the fireplace. Why could I never please anyone? Eliza, who was headstrong and selfish, was respected.

50
00:06:04.681 --> 00:06:16.175
Georgiana, who had a spoiled temperament, was always indulged. My head still ached and bled from the blow I'd received from John.

51
00:06:16.176 --> 00:06:25.573
Why did no one punish him? I knew if I were a happy, handsome, romping child, everyone would treat me better.

52
00:06:25.574 --> 00:06:30.930
It was past four o'clock, and daylight began to fade from the red room.

53
00:06:30.931 --> 00:06:39.872
Rain beat on the windows and wind howled in the trees. I started to think of Mr. Reed, my mother's brother.

54
00:06:39.873 --> 00:06:51.649
He had taken me in when I was an infant without parents. In his last moments, he'd made Mrs. Reed promise to rear me as one of her own children.

55
00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:56.974
I never doubted that my uncle would have treated me kindly if he'd lived.

56
00:06:56.975 --> 00:07:04.378
Then I began to recall stories I'd heard about dead men, troubled by the violation of their last wishes.

57
00:07:04.379 --> 00:07:15.804
Suddenly a light gleamed on the wall. I shrieked and rushed to the door, desperately pounding on it. Bessie and Miss Abbot came running.

58
00:07:15.805 --> 00:07:20.285
"Why are you screaming?" asked Bessie, opening the door.

59
00:07:20.286 --> 00:07:25.455
"Let me out!" I cried. "I think I saw a ghost!"

60
00:07:25.456 --> 00:07:30.779
"I know the girl's naughty tricks," Miss Abbot said in disgust.

61
00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:40.273
"What's all this?" demanded Mrs. Reed, walking into the room. "I said Jane was to stay here alone."

62
00:07:40.274 --> 00:07:45.422
"But she screamed so loud," pleaded Bessie, holding my hand.

63
00:07:45.423 --> 00:07:52.349
"I despise trickery," said Mrs. Reed. "You will stay here an extra hour."

64
00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:55.091
"Oh, Aunt, have pity, let me—"

65
00:07:55.092 --> 00:08:03.501
"Silence!" commanded Mrs. Reed. Ignoring my wild sobs, she abruptly locked me in again.

66
00:08:03.502 --> 00:08:11.560
I heard her walking away, and then, dear reader, I soon lost consciousness.

